inline_comments.comment:
Runtime crash when any of the three new status values arrive in a test run. `statusValueToStoryIdsMap` is initialized with only the five `TestStatusValue` keys (`pending`, `success`, `error`, `warning`, `unknown`). When a status entry has `value: 'status-value:new'` (or `modified`/`affected`), `statusValueToStoryIdsMap['status-value:new']` is `undefined`; calling `.push()` on `undefined` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'push')`. The `as TestStatusValue` cast silences TypeScript but does not prevent the crash.

Fix with a membership guard before the push:
```ts
const key = status.value as TestStatusValue;
if (key in statusValueToStoryIdsMap) {
  statusValueToStoryIdsMap[key].push(status.storyId);
}
```

general_comments.comment:
The `showBranchStatus` inline array `['status-value:modified', 'status-value:affected', 'status-value:new', 'status-value:warning', 'status-value:error'] as StatusValue[]` in `Tree.tsx` is allocated on every render. Because this set now expands with the new values, it is worth extracting to a module-level `Set<StatusValue>` constant and replacing `.includes(status)` with `.has(status)`. This avoids the per-render allocation, eliminates the `as StatusValue[]` cast, and gives O(1) lookup:

```ts
const BRANCH_STATUS_VALUES = new Set<StatusValue>([
  'status-value:modified',
  'status-value:affected',
  'status-value:new',
  'status-value:warning',
  'status-value:error',
]);
// …
const showBranchStatus = BRANCH_STATUS_VALUES.has(status);
```
